{
	title: 'Markdown in model text; a streaming block never shrinks',
	spec: `Model text (text blocks, not thinking or tool output) is rendered as markdown in both clients, from one parser in src/common so the terminal and the web never disagree. Supported: fenced code blocks (kept exactly as written, language shown), tables, # headings, lists, > quotes, and inline **bold**, *italic*, \`code\` and [links](url). A few HTML entities are decoded outside code (&nbsp; &amp; &lt; &gt; &quot;), so a line holding only &nbsp;, which models write to force a blank line, becomes a blank row. The terminal draws styles with SGR and aligns table columns; the web builds real elements (a <table> for tables), and model text never reaches the page as HTML: everything is escaped before markup, so a reply containing <script> or <img onerror> shows as text. Budget: about 300 non-test lines for parser and both renderers. Old Hal is the reference (artifacts old-markdown.ts, old-md.ts, old-web-markdown.ts and their tests); copy its behaviour, not its structure.

A streaming block never shrinks, only grows (the user saw the web scroll position tremble as a streaming table grew and shrank). Two parts. (1) The parser is kind to prefixes: a trailing line that could start or continue a table (it begins with |) is held back until the next line arrives, a table is drawn only once its separator row has arrived, and every construct opens optimistically and is closed at the end of the input: an opening ** or * or ` hides its marker and styles what follows at once, an open ``` fence is a code block to the end. A guess that turns out wrong (the marker was a literal, as in 2**3 or a paragraph ending with no closing marker) shows the marker again, which only adds width. Only a marker that can open (followed by a non-space, CommonMark left-flanking) opens; one at the very end of the input, whose next character is not known yet, is held back until it is. Inline styles never run past the end of their paragraph or cell. (2) A safety net that guarantees the rule whatever the renderer does: each client keeps, for the one block that is streaming, the tallest height it has drawn it at, and pads it to that height (terminal: blank rows at its end; web: min-height on the card). The high-water mark belongs to that block only, lives in memory, starts from the first render of the block (also after a reconnect or restart), and is dropped when the block stops streaming; then the block takes its natural height once. It is never derived from other blocks or tabs and never persisted (a high-water mark of painted height, peak, once put the terminal prompt mid-screen after a restart; see task vq).`,
	status: 'planned',
	needs: [],
	foldInto: [
		'cc',
		'1b'
	],
	notes: [
		'Old web markdown (old-web-markdown.ts) builds an HTML string with markdown.escape first and sets innerHTML; its /status usage-table special case (usageCell, meters) is not wanted here. Either escape-then-markup or DOM building is fine; the test is that <script>, <img onerror> and a javascript: link in model text stay inert.',
		"Where it plugs in: terminal model text is drawn in src/client/item-view.ts (case 'text') via frame.itemRows; the Hal cursor (task 5p) is appended to the last streamed row by withCursor in frame.ts, so the renderer must keep the last row's visible width known. Web model text is src/web/components/Card.tsx. The streaming block is the one 5p calls streaming (t.live, running, last item is the live turn's last text block).",
		'Why shrinking happens, so the tests aim right: a | line shown as plain text wraps differently once it becomes a table cell; the separator row appears or disappears; a new wide row widens a column and earlier rows rewrap into fewer rows (web table layout does this by itself); closing ** removes 4 visible chars. Test the rule by feeding one reply a character at a time and checking each frame is no shorter than the one before (terminal rows; web: the card min-height), for a reply with a table, a code fence and bold text.'
	]
}
